# Electronic Modulation of Metal–Organic Frameworks by Interfacial Bridging for Efficient pH‐Universal Hydrogen Evolution

## abstract
Designing well‐defined interfacial chemical bond bridges is an effective strategy to optimize the catalytic activity of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), but it remains challenging. Herein, a facile in situ growth strategy is reported for the synthesis of tightly connected 2D/2D heterostructures by coupling MXene with CoBDC nanosheets. The multifunctional MXene nanosheets with high conductivity and ideal hydrophilicity as bridging carriers can ensure structural stability and sufficient exposure to active sites. Moreover, the Co–O–Ti bond bridging formed at the interface effectively triggers the charge transfer and modulates the electronic structure of the Co‐active site, which enhances the reaction kinetics. As a result, the optimized CoBDC/MXene exhibits superior hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) activity with low overpotentials of 29, 41, and 76 mV at 10 mA cm−2 in alkaline, acidic, and neutral electrolytes, respectively, which is comparable to commercial Pt/C. Theoretical calculation demonstrates that the interfacial bridging‐induced electron redistribution optimizes the free energy of water dissociation and hydrogen adsorption, resulting in improved hydrogen evolution. This study not only provides a novel electrocatalyst for efficient HER at all pH conditions but also opens up a new avenue for designing highly active catalytic systems.

Designing well-defined interfacial chemical bond bridges is an effective strategy to optimize the catalytic activity of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), but it remains challenging. Herein, a facile in situ growth strategy is reported for the synthesis of tightly connected 2D/2D heterostructures by coupling MXene with CoBDC nanosheets. The multifunctional MXene nanosheets with high conductivity and ideal hydrophilicity as bridging carriers can ensure structural stability and sufficient exposure to active sites. Moreover, the Co–O–Ti bond bridging formed at the interface effectively triggers the charge transfer and modulates the electronic structure of the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{c o}}$ -active site, which enhances the reaction kinetics. As a result, the optimized CoBDC/MXene exhibits superior hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) activity with low overpotentials of 29, 41, and $76m v$ at $\mathsf{10\ m A\ c m^{-2}}$ in alkaline, acidic, and neutral electrolytes, respectively, which is comparable to commercial Pt/C. Theoretical calculation demonstrates that the interfacial bridging-induced electron redistribution optimizes the free energy of water dissociation and hydrogen adsorption, resulting in improved hydrogen evolution. This study not only provides a novel electrocatalyst for efficient HER at all pH conditions but also opens up a new avenue for designing highly active catalytic systems.
## 1. Introduction
Hydrogen is considered as one of the most promising alternatives to fossil fuels as a clean energy carrier with zero carbon emissions, which facilitates the goal of carbon neutrality.[1–3] Electrocatalytic water splitting is an attractive route for green hydrogen production.[4–6] Platinum (Pt)- based catalysts are generally regarded as the most effective electrocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) due to their excellent catalytic performance.[7–9] However, the scarcity and high cost of $\mathrm{Pt}.$ based materials have limited large-scale practical applications.[10,11] In addition, most HER catalysts can only work under acidic or alkaline conditions, which is difficult to adapt to the change of electrolyte concentration during water electrolysis.[12–15] Therefore, the rational design of non-noble metal electrocatalysts for pH-universal HER is highly attractive but remains a great challenge.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have received growing interest in electrocatalysis owing to their periodic porous structures, high specific surface areas, and tunable chemical compositions.[16–20] In particular, 2D MOFs nanosheets are expected to provide abundant exposed active sites as well as fast mass transport.[21–26] However, MOFs have poor electrical conductivity and low intrinsic activity of metal centers, dramatically limiting their applications as electrocatalysts.[27–30] Generally, loading MOFs on highly conductive supports to form compact heterostructures is an effective approach to enhance conductivity and catalytic activity.[31] Moreover, chemical bond bridges in the interface can trigger fast electron transfer, which can optimize the HER free energy and lead to accelerated reaction kinetics.[32,33] It has been widely investigated in many electrocatalytic processes for realizing new functions that are difficult to achieve by any individual material.[34,35] However, the modulation of active sites based on interfacial bond bridging formed by traditional metal oxide and hydroxide substrates is unsatisfactory to maintain durability in extreme catalytic environments.[36,37] Meanwhile, the complex surface composition of conventional supports seriously hinders the exploration of the structure-activity relationship and catalytic mechanism.[38] Therefore, developing novel carriers to optimize the metal active centers of MOFs is a critical but challenging task. In this regard, semimetal 2D MXene exhibits efficient charge transport, rich surface chemistry, and superior corrosion resistance to ensure that the catalysts maintain structural stability and catalytic activity in complex electrocatalytic environments.[39–44] The superior 2D/2D heterostructure of MXene combined with MOF is expected to generate unique electronic coupling properties. In addition, the interfacial bridges formed by MXene and metal sites can tune the Fermi level of the active center, resulting in enhanced intrinsic activity compared to conventional catalyst substrates.[32,34,45] Despite so many advantages, the influence of the interfacial microstructure-activity relationship between MXene and MOFs for the HER catalytic process is so far rarely reported. Therefore, the reciprocal decoration between multifunctional MXene and 2D MOFs is expected to be an efficient pH-universal electrocatalyst for hydrogen evolution.
## 2. Results and Discussion
As illustrated in Figure  1a, a facile ultrasound-assisted in situ growth strategy is employed to synthesize CoBDC/MXene nanosheets with a 2D/2D heterostructure. Briefly, MXene $(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}})$ nanosheets are prepared by selectively etching the Al element in the MAX phase $(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2})$ and subsequent ultrasonic treatment (Figure  S1, Supporting Information). Compared with the pristine $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ material, the XRD of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ MXene exhibits a typical peak at $6.1^{\circ}$ corresponding to the (002) plane, indicating that $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ has been completely converted to $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ MXene (Figures  S2 and S3, Supporting Information). Subsequently, the electropositive cobalt ions are electrostatically adsorbed onto the electronegative MXene nanosheets with surface functional groups and 2D CoBDC nanostructures are in situ grown on MXene (CoBDC/MXene) after the addition of terephthalic acid. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images demonstrate that CoBDC nanosheets interconnected with each other are uniformly and completely dispersed on the entire surface of the MXene to form a 2D/2D heterostructure (Figure  1b). As shown in Figure  1c, the nanoscale sheetlike structure with smooth surfaces and curved edges can be clearly observed consistent with the pure CoBDC morphology (Figure  S4, Supporting Information), confirming the combination of CoBDC with MXene. Notably, the perfect dispersion of CoBDC nanosheets on MXene can provide abundant electrode/electrolyte contact interface and shorten the ion diffusion path, leading to fast electrochemical kinetics. Meanwhile, the 2D/2D heterostructure with a high exposed surface area is beneficial for the storage of a large number of charges, which endows the catalyst with robust cycling ability. The thickness of CoBDC/MXene nanosheets was identified by the atomic force microscopy (AFM) to be ${\approx}14.2~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image reveals that ultrathin CoBDC nanosheets with clear outlines ideally adhere to MXene (Figure  1d). The presence of MXene is identified in the white dashed region (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The interlayer spacing of MXene ${\approx}0.97$ nm can be distinguished in the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Figure  1e). Consistently, Figure  1f presents tightly continuous bright and dark interfaces corresponding to CoBDC and MXene, respectively, directly demonstrating the formation of heterostructures with high-quality interfacial contacts. The lattice spacing of $0.26\ \mathrm{nm}$ corresponds to the (100) plane of MXene. As shown in Figure  1g, the TEM elemental mapping of Ti and Co indicates that CoBDC nanosheets grow on the MXene surface. This further verifies the formation of CoBDC/ MXene heterostructures. Moreover, the main peaks in the XRD pattern of CoBDC/MXene can be well assigned to the mixed MXene and CoBDC phases, which also verifies the formation of CoBDC/MXene nanostructures. The Brunauer-EmmettTeller (BET) surface area of CoBDC/MXene $(72.70\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is much larger than that of CoBDC $(19.13\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ and MXene $(16.91\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , indicating that the 2D/2D composite structure increases the exposure of catalyst active sites (Figure  S7, Supporting Information).[46] The above results confirm the successful preparation of CoBDC/MXene catalysts with welldefined 2D/2D heterostructures.
The interfacial structure and electronic properties of CoBDC/ MXene are explored by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure  S8, Supporting Information). Compared with the O 1s spectrum of CoBDC (Figure  2a), the oxygen vacancy peak completely disappears in CoBDC/MXene, while the ratio of metal–oxygen bonds increases significantly.[47] This implies that the unsaturated coordinated Co sites in CoBDC connect with Ti–O bonds in MXene to form Co–O–Ti bridges at the heterostructure interface between CoBDC and MXene. The weaker EPR signal of CoBDC/MXene compared with CoBDC further confirms that the unsaturated Co sites are bridged with MXene to form ${\mathrm{Co-O-Ti}}$ bonds (Figure  S9, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the O 1s spectrum of CoBDC/MXene shifts to high binding energy, which can be attributed to the formation of ${\mathrm{Co-O-Ti}}$ bonds. As for the Co 2p spectrum of CoBDC/MXene (Figure 2b), the peaks of Co $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ can be observed at binding energies of 781.67 and $797.53\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, as well as two satellite peaks.[48] In addition, the high-resolution peaks at 454.59/461.15 and 457.87 eV in the Ti 2p spectrum of CoBDC/ MXene (Figure  2c) are attributed to Ti–C and Ti–O bonds, respectively. Remarkably, the binding energy of Co 2p for CoBDC/MXene exhibits a positive shift compared to CoBDC, indicating the reduction of electron density for Co. In contrast, the binding energy of Ti 2p in CoBDC/MXene shifts to a lower value than that of MXene. This result indicates that the Co–O– Ti bridges formed in the CoBDC/MXene heterostructure interface can induce electron transfer from Co to Ti sites through O atoms, which effectively optimizes the charge distribution of the catalyst.
X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) analysis is employed to further understand the local electronic state and reveal the formation of Co–O–Ti bridges. As illustrated in Figure  2d, the Co K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra show that the absorption edge of CoBDC/MXene shifts to higher energy compared with CoBDC, indicating that the immobilization of CoBDC on MXene reduces the electron density around the Co sites.[49] This is consistent with the results observed in XPS. The valence state of Co atoms in CoBDC/ MXene is further estimated by the first derivative curves and the fitted average oxidation states from the Co K-edge XANES.[50] As shown in Figure  2e, the oxidation state of Co in the CoBDC/MXene (1.68) composite is between Co foil and CoO and higher than that of CoBDC (1.49) (Figure  2f), which is consistent with their XANES results. The elevated valence state of Co sites is caused by Co-O-Ti bridging at the heterostructure interface, which can accelerate charge/ion transfer during HER and enhance the intrinsic activity of the electrocatalyst. In addition, the oscillation of the Co K-edge EXAFS function with significant amplitude change suggests different local atomic arrangements of CoBDC/MXene and CoBDC (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The Co K-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra of CoBDC/ MXene (Figure 2g) show that the main peak is attributed to the single scattering paths of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-O}$ shell and the relatively weak peaks are assigned to the higher shell contribution of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-C}$ and Co–O–Co/Ti.[23,51] Notably, the characteristic peak intensity of the Co–O–Co/Ti bonds in CoBDC/MXene is enhanced compared with CoBDC, further verifying the formation of Co–O–Ti bridges.[52] Moreover, the positive peak shift of the $_{\mathsf{C o-O-C o}/}$ Ti bond implies a strong interaction at the CoBDC/MXene heterostructure interface. The results are also confirmed by the wavelet transform spectroscopic analysis of Co K-edge EXAFS (Figure  2h). Based on the above results, the formation and electron transfer mechanism of CoBDC/MXene with interfacial Co–O–Ti bridging are demonstrated in Figure  2i. Therefore, the XPS and XANES analysis synergistically verify the existence of interfacial Co–O–Ti bridging in CoBDC/MXene, which can change the local electronic configuration of the catalyst and optimize the adsorption/desorption process of reaction intermediates, resulting in enhanced electrocatalytic performance. Besides the changes of CoBDC electronic structure caused by MXene, the surface properties of electrocatalysts can also be significantly improved. The influence of MXene on the surface wettability of CoBDC was investigated by a contact angle test. The contact angles of CoBDC and CoBDC/MXene are $82^{\circ}$ and $0^{\circ}$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information), respectively, indicating that the wettability of the catalysts is significantly enhanced after MXene modification. This ideal hydrophilicity is beneficial to improving the interaction between the catalyst and the electrolyte, resulting in more active sites exposed in the electrolyte to enhance the HER performance.
Inspired by the unique electronic structure of the CoBDC/ MXene electrocatalyst, the HER electrocatalytic performance was measured in $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH using a standard three-electrode system. As displayed in Figure  3a, the optimized CoBDC/ MXene exhibits a lower overpotential of $29~\mathrm{mV}$ than that of CoBDC $(174\mathrm{mV})$ ) and MXene $(242~\mathrm{mV})$ to achieve $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for HER even better than commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ at large current densities (Figures S12 and S13, Supporting Information). Strikingly, CoBDC/MXene is also a superior HER catalyst compared to most recently reported catalysts using $1.0\mathrm{{M}}$ KOH as electrolyte (Figure 3h; Table S1, Supporting Information). The Tafel slope of CoBDC/MXene $(46\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ is close to $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ ( $39\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ , implying fast HER kinetics caused by the interaction between CoBDC and MXene (Figure  3b,c).[6] This may be attributed to the formation of hydronium ions on the catalyst surface under alkaline conditions, resulting in high catalytic activity and low Tafel.[53] Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed to investigate the charge transfer properties (Figure  S14, Supporting Information). The Nyquist plot presents a semicircular shape, which can identify the electrode process as a control step. The fitting results of the equivalent circuit model show that CoBDC/MXene $(24.36~\Omega)$ provides lower charge transfer resistance than CoBDC $(33.90~\Omega)$ , indicating faster electron transport (Table S2, Supporting Information).[54] Meanwhile, the exchange current density of the electrocatalyst is obtained by extrapolating the Tafel plot. The exchange current density of CoBDC/MXene is $1.03\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ higher than that of CoBDC $(0.38\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}},$ ) and MXene $(0.08\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}.$ ), which further verified its superior HER activity (Figure  S15, Supporting Information). In addition, the double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ is calculated to be 11.60, 4.36, and $1.63~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ for CoBDC/ MXene, CoBDC, and MXene, respectively (Figure  S16, Supporting Information). The ECSA value of CoBDC/MXene is ${\approx}2.66$ times higher than that of CoBDC, which is attributed to the abundant active sites exposed by the 2D/2D heterostructure (Table S3, Supporting Information). Obviously, CoBDC/MXene has the largest electrochemically active surface area, suggesting the special 2D/2D structure in the CoBDC/MXene heterostructure indeed exposes more active sites.
To further explore the improved HER performance, the turnover frequency (TOF) was calculated to evaluate the intrinsic hydrogen-evolving activity of the CoBDC/MXene electrocatalyst. Specifically, the TOF of CoBDC/MXene is $0.226\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at an overpotential of $100~\mathrm{{mV},}$ , which is ${\approx}56$ times that of CoBDC $(0.004~\mathrm{s^{-1}})$ (Figure 3d; Table S4, Supporting Information). This result suggests that the intrinsic activity of CoBDC/MXene is improved by interfacial Co-O-Ti bridging, leading to more prominent catalytic performance. Besides the electrochemical activity, the long-term operational stability of the CoBDC/MXene catalyst is an important factor to evaluate the potential for practical applications. In Figure  3e, CoBDC/MXene exhibits negligible activity decay after 3000 cycles. In contrast, the HER activity of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ declines significantly. Specifically, the HER performance of CoBDC/MXene displays only a $10\%$ decrease (Figure 3f), whereas a 2.8-fold decay rate for commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ can be observed after continuous 3000 cycles. As a key criterion for practical application, the stability of the CoBDC/MXene is investigated by galvanostatic measurement, which shows negligible potential fluctuations after testing at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for $50\mathrm{{h}}$ (Figure 3g). CoBDC/MXene still exhibits superior HER stability at large current densities (Figure S17, Supporting Information). After the durability test, the nanosheet structure is well maintained to the original morphology and the XPS spectra exhibit no detectable change, which further verifies the catalytic stability during operation (Figures S19–S21, Supporting Information). Moreover, the Faradaic efficiency of the CoBDC/MXene electrocatalyst for HER is close to $100\%$ (Figure  S22, Supporting Information). The above results demonstrate that the 2D/2D CoBDC/MXene heterostructure with interfacial bridging possesses efficient electron transport and facile kinetics for HER, leading to extraordinary catalytic activity and stability in 1 M KOH.
To understand the effect of interfacial Co-O-Ti bridging on HER performance, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed on MXene, CoBDC, and CoBDC/MXene systems (Figure S32, Supporting Information). The charge density difference of CoBDC/MXene is visually plotted in Figure  5a to clearly understand the influence of Co-O-Ti bridging on the surface electronic states. Apparently, CoBDC exhibits electron transfer from Co to Ti atoms after MXene modification (Figure  S33, Supporting Information), which is attributed to the formation of interfacial Co-O-Ti bridges establishing continuous electron transport between the two phases. According to the density of states (DOS) in Figure 5b, the CoBDC/MXene heterostructure shows a high electron density at the Fermi level compared to the single CoBDC and MXene. This indicates that the presence of coupled electron orbitals at the heterostructure interface between CoBDC and MXene optimizes the conductivity of CoBDC/MXene and reduces the charge transfer resistance. The d-band center of Co in CoBDC/MXene $(-2.12)$ shifts to lower energy compared to pure CoBDC (−1.95; Figure  5c), indicating weaker adsorption of H intermediates on the CoBDC/MXene interface and promoting the subsequent evolution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .[55] The above results suggest that the formation of interfacial bridges can regulate the electronic structure, leading to the redistribution of charges.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, we have developed a facile in situ growth strategy to fabricate a novel 2D/2D CoBDC/MXene heterostructure with modulated electronic structure, presenting high-performance HER electrocatalytic activity at all pH values. The robust 2D/2D heterostructure endows the CoBDC/MXene electrocatalyst with highly exposed active sites and favorable mass transfer pathways. The formation of interfacial $_{\mathrm{Co-O-Ti}}$ bridges can facilitate the electronic modification of heterostructures by charge relocation, which is a key factor to achieve high catalytic performance. Moreover, Co–O–Ti bridges with Gibbs free energy modulation can optimize water dissociation and adsorption/desorption of hydrogen intermediates, enhancing the intrinsic activity of HER. In particular, the CoBDC/MXene catalyst enables a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ with low overpotentials of 29, 41, and $76~\mathrm{mV}$ in alkaline, acidic, and neutral electrolytes, respectively. Our study provides valuable insights into the modulation of electron configurations in hybrid electrocatalysts and facilitates the rational design and controllable synthesis of high-performance catalysts for sustainable hydrogen production.
